  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 4,
    "lesson_title": "Real Poles",
    "lesson_core_move": "Infer from each real pole of a transfer function whether the corresponding LTI response mode is a decaying or growing nonoscillatory exponential.",
    "section_count": 4,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Real Poles",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Real Poles",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "A transfer function written as a ratio of polynomials links pole values to natural response modes: each denominator root produces an exponential term in the system response. For this lesson, your target move is to read a pole value $p$ from denominator form and map it to the mode shape it creates.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the simplest form first: $H(s)=\\frac{A}{s-p}$. The denominator is zero when $s=p$, so the pole is at $s=p$, and the corresponding time-domain mode is the canonical term $A e^{pt}u(t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "H(s)=\\frac{K}{s-p} \\Rightarrow h(t)=Ke^{pt}u(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now apply the same direct rule to the base example. For $H(s)=\\frac{A}{s+2}$, the pole is $p=-2$. Because $p=-2<0$, the mode is $e^{-2t}u(t)$, which decreases as $t$ increases.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "p<0 \\Rightarrow e^{pt}\\text{ decays}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the same comparison for other signs: if $p>0$, then $e^{pt}$ grows, and if $p=0$, then the mode is constant over time.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "p>0 \\Rightarrow e^{pt}\\text{ grows}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For real poles, this gives a direct stability read: negative real poles produce decaying nonoscillatory modes, positive real poles produce growing nonoscillatory modes, and $p=0$ gives a sustained constant mode.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Real Poles",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Interpreting Negative Real Poles as Decaying Modes",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Interpret the real-pole mode in $H(s)=\\frac{3}{s+5}$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Read the denominator of the transfer function first: $H(s)=\\frac{3}{s+5}$ has $D(s)=s+5$. Write it in root form as $s-(-5)$, so the real pole is $p=-5$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "s+5=s-(-5)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Map the pole to the homogeneous-mode rule from the tutorial: for a real pole at $p$, the time-domain mode is $h_h(t)=Ce^{pt}u(t)$. Substituting $p=-5$ gives the mode from this factor.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "h_h(t)=3e^{-5t}u(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Because the pole value is negative ($p=-5<0$), the factor is decaying and nonoscillatory: it contributes $e^{-5t}$, which shrinks in magnitude as $t$ increases.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "p<0\\Rightarrow e^{pt}\\text{ is decaying and nonoscillatory}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Real Poles",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Interpreting Positive Real Poles as Growing Modes",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Interpret the real-pole mode in $H(s)=\\\\frac{1}{s-1.5}$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Read the transfer function denominator: for $H(s)=\\frac{1}{s-1.5}$, the denominator is $D(s)=s-1.5$, so the real pole is at $p=1.5$. The pole is positive because $1.5>0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "D(s)=s-1.5 = s-(1.5) \\Rightarrow p=1.5",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Apply the same mapping used before for real poles: a real pole at $p$ produces a nonoscillatory mode $h_h(t)=Ce^{pt}u(t)$. Here, use $p=1.5$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "h_h(t)=Ce^{1.5t}u(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now compare the time trend: because $p>0$, the factor $e^{1.5t}$ grows without bound as $t$ increases. So this is a growing, nonoscillatory mode, and it is unstable if it is the dominant real mode.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "p>0 \\Rightarrow e^{pt}=e^{1.5t}\\ \\text{grows}\\ \\text{as}\\ t\\to\\infty",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Real Poles",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Comparing Real-Pole Locations and Exponential Rates",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Example: Compare the nonoscillatory rates for transfer functions with poles $s=-1$, $s=-3$, and $s=2$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use a concrete transfer function with those poles, for example $H(s)=\\frac{1}{(s+1)(s+3)(s-2)}$. The real poles are the roots of the denominator: $p_1=-1$, $p_2=-3$, and $p_3=2$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "D(s)=(s+1)(s+3)(s-2)=0 \\Rightarrow p\\in\\{-1,-3,2\\}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Convert each real pole to its natural-mode exponent using the same real-pole rule: for pole $p$, the nonoscillatory mode is $e^{pt}u(t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "p=-3 \\Rightarrow h_1(t)=Ce^{-3t}u(t),\\quad p=-1 \\Rightarrow h_2(t)=Ce^{-t}u(t),\\quad p=2 \\Rightarrow h_3(t)=Ce^{2t}u(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now rank the exponents. The negative poles both decay: $e^{-3t}$ is faster-decaying than $e^{-t}$, so $e^{-3t}u(t)$ is the fastest decay and $e^{-t}u(t)$ is the slowest decay. The positive pole $p=2$ gives $e^{2t}u(t)$, which grows and dominates at large $t$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "-3<-1<0<2 \\Rightarrow e^{-3t}\\text{ decays fastest},\\ e^{-t}\\text{ decays slowest among decays},\\ e^{2t}\\text{ dominates}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
